	select_type		|	The type of select operation being preformed. Simple: no subqueries	 				   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		table		|	The target table being accessed.									 				   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
	partitions		|Table partitions that are being scanned, in this case none, it's not a partitioned table. |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		type		|	How mysql accesses the rows. range: Scans index records.							   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
possible_keys		|	List of indexes considered to find rows												   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		key			|	The key index MySQL chose to execute												   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
	key_len			|	The length in bytes of the index the mySQL used										   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		ref			|	Shows which collumns or constants are compared to the used index					   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		rows		|	The number of rows mySQL believes it looked at to return the result set				   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
	filtered		|Percentage of the rows fetched via index that will satisfy the remaining WHERE conditions |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		Extra		|	Additional execution details and flags. 											   |
--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|

Extra flags:
1. Using index: THe query uses a Covering Index. All the requested data is satisfied entierely from the index without reading the table rows on disk.

2. Using index condition: MySQL reads index records first to test WHERE clauses before accessing the table rows.

3. Using where: A WHERE clause is filtering rows after reading them from the table.

4. Using filesort: MySQL must preform an extra pass to sort the results.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

After looking at this information I can see that my biggest bottle neck is in the sorting. I see that my querry used filesort. So, I will add a composite index so that the index may be used for sorting and no filesort will be needed.

CREATE INDEX idx_city_id ON rets_property (L_City, id DESC);

id DESC will pre-sort the results by id and eliminate the need for filesort.
This index will be used any time a city is filtered by.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

